# NOBORU SECURITY RULE

Version: 1.0

Priority: CRITICAL

Status: AUTHORITATIVE

This rule governs authentication, authorization, middleware, session handling, and security-sensitive implementation within Noboru.

Every auth flow, protected route, token handling path, and permission check must follow this document and the security requirements in architecture.mdc.

⸻

PURPOSE

The purpose of this rule is to ensure:

* User data remains protected by default
* Authentication is consistent and verifiable
* Authorization is enforced at every boundary
* Secrets never enter source control or client bundles
* Security failures are explicit, logged, and non-leaking
* Noboru remains trustworthy as a long-term learning platform

Security is not optional infrastructure.

It is part of domain design.

⸻

REQUIREMENTS

Authentication

* Supabase Auth is the authentication source
* Session validation must occur server-side for protected operations
* features/authentication/ owns auth workflows, types, and presentation
* Middleware in middleware.ts protects routes according to established patterns

Authorization

* Every user-owned resource requires ownership validation
* Admin actions require explicit role checks
* Never trust client-provided userId, role, or ownership flags without verification
* RLS must protect database rows; application checks remain mandatory

Row Level Security

* Required on all user-owned tables
* Policies must default to least privilege
* Service role usage is server-only and narrowly scoped

Secrets

* Forbidden: API keys, service role keys, JWT secrets, or credentials in code
* Required: environment variables via .env.local and deployment secret stores
* Never log tokens, refresh tokens, passwords, or recovery codes
* Never expose service role keys to client bundles

Environment Variables

* Public variables only for explicitly public values such as anon keys where required
* Server-only secrets loaded only in server contexts
* Document required env vars in deployment docs, not in committed secrets files

Input Safety

* Validate and sanitize input at API and service boundaries
* Reject unexpected fields when schemas are used
* Avoid dynamic SQL outside controlled repository patterns

Transport and Cookies

* Use secure cookie settings where auth cookies are involved
* Follow framework and Supabase recommendations for session storage

Error Handling

* Return generic auth failure messages to clients
* Log detailed failures server-side without sensitive payloads

Architecture Alignment

* UI must not access database directly
* Repositories must scope queries by authenticated user where applicable
* Security logic belongs in services, middleware, and policies—not scattered in components

⸻

CODING STANDARDS

Middleware

* Keep middleware focused on route protection and session presence
* Avoid heavy business logic in middleware
* Fail closed: unauthenticated users do not reach protected handlers

Auth Feature

* Centralize login, logout, signup, recovery, and session refresh workflows
* Typed auth states: loading, authenticated, unauthenticated, error, recovery
* Never store plaintext passwords outside auth provider flow

API Routes

* Verify session before mutations
* Map auth failures to 401 and permission failures to 403
* Do not leak whether a protected resource exists when unauthorized

Client Code

* Use anon key only with RLS-protected operations
* Never embed service role or private keys in client-side code

Logging

* Log auth anomalies with request context
* Redact tokens and credentials from all logs

Dependencies

* Keep auth utilities in authentication feature or shared lib/security as established
* Do not duplicate permission checks with inconsistent rules

⸻

SUCCESS CRITERIA

Security work is successful when:

* Protected routes cannot be accessed without valid session verification
* User-owned data is protected by RLS and service checks
* No secrets appear in git history or client bundles
* Authorization failures are handled consistently
* Auth flows support error and recovery states
* Middleware and authentication feature ownership is clear
* Security behavior is testable at service and route boundaries

⸻

FAILURE CRITERIA

Security is compromised or degraded when any of the following occur:

* Secrets committed to the repository
* Client-side service role usage
* Missing RLS on user-owned tables
* Trusting client-sent user IDs for mutations
* Authorization enforced only in UI
* Tokens or passwords logged to console or telemetry
* Public env vars used for private credentials
* Inconsistent permission checks across routes
* Detailed internal errors returned to untrusted clients

When failure criteria appear, treat as release-blocking until remediated.

⸻

EXAMPLES

Correct protected mutation

API route verifies session, resolves userId from auth context, calls service with that userId.

Correct middleware

middleware.ts redirects unauthenticated users away from protected app sections.

Correct env usage

process.env.SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY used only in server repository infrastructure.

Incorrect client code

Importing service role key into a React component.

Incorrect authorization

if (body.userId === profile.id) without session verification.

Incorrect logging

console.log({ refreshToken }) during auth debugging left in production path.

⸻

ANTI-PATTERNS

Secrets in source code

Authorization checks only in components

Bypassing RLS assumptions because "the UI hides the button"

Logging full auth responses

Shared admin endpoints without role validation

Using security through obscurity for protected resources

Copy-pasted permission logic with divergent behavior

Exposing stack traces on auth failures

Hardcoded test credentials in non-test production paths

Client-side-only route protection without server enforcement

